  "id": "DrugBank_DB00258 - MONDO_1567",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Acetate is associated with Nephrocalcinosis through a mechanism involving calcium metabolism and its effects on kidney function. The evidence suggests that Calcium Acetate is linked to conditions that lead to calcium overload, such as Phosphorus Metabolism Disease and Cardiac Arrest, which in turn are connected to Nephrocalcinosis. These pathways imply that Calcium Acetate may contribute to the formation of calcium deposits in the kidneys by promoting excessive calcium retention or altering calcium homeostasis. The indirect links through different metabolic pathways highlight a potential role of Calcium Acetate in kidney calcification, though the exact mechanism remains partially understood. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of calcium metabolism in the development of Nephrocalcinosis, suggesting that Calcium Acetate may be a contributing factor in this condition.",
